.TH SLICE 1L "1987 September 14" "Cognos Inc.".SH NAMEslice \- split a file into pieces, by pattern or every n'th line.SH SYNOPSIS.B slice.tr _-[\|\fB_f\ \fIfilename\fP\|][\|\fB_i\fP\fIn\fP\|][\|\fB_a\fP\|][\|\fB_x\fP\|][\|\fB_r\fP\ \fIrejectfile\fP\|][\|\fB_\&m\fP\||\ \fB_\&s\fP\||\ \fB_\&n\fP\fIn\fP\|][\|\fIformat\fP\ .\|.\|.\|].LP.sp.B slice[\|\fB_f\ \fIfilename\fP\|][\|\fB_i\fP\fIn\fP\|][\|\fB_a\fP\|][\|\fB_x\fP\|][\|\fB_r\fP\ \fIrejectfile\fP\|]\ \fB_\&e\ \fIexpression\fP\ [\|\fIformat\fP\ .\|.\|.\|].LP.sp.B slice[\|\fB_f\ \fIfilename\fP\|][\|\fB_i\fP\fIn\fP\|][\|\fB_a\fP\|][\|\fB_x\fP\|][\|\fB_r\fP\ \fIrejectfile\fP\|]\ \fIexpression\fP\ [\|\fIformat\fP\ .\|.\|.\|].tr.SH DESCRIPTION.I Slicesplits a file into pieces (called \fIslices\fR) which are depositedinto one or more output files.  The output files are named accordingto the \fIformat\fR strings provided.  The input file is splitwhenever a pattern is matched or every \fIn\fR lines, depending on theoptions selected.  Because some of the options are mutually exclusive,there are three forms of the command..LPWhenever a pattern match is used to slice the file, lines occurringbefore the first match are sent to the \fIreject\fR file (which is\fI/dev/null\fR by default).  Lines are also rejected when an output filecannot be opened, for whatever reason..LP.SH OPTIONS.LP.IP "\fB-f\fR \fIfilename\fR"Input for \fIslice\fR is taken from the named file rather than \fIstdin\fR..IP "\fB-i\fP\fIn\fP"The starting number for numbering output files generated by formatscontaining "#\&n" (see \fISubstitutions\fR below).  The defaultstarting number is one..IP "\fB-a\fR"Causes the file to be split \fBafter\fR the line matching the pattern,rather than before (as is normally the case).  This option will notwork properly if an output format contains the substitution parameters"#\&1", "#\&2", etc.  (See \fIformat\fR below)..IP "\fB-x\fR"Causes the matched line to be excluded from the output files.  Handyfor eliminating cut lines, etc..IP "\fB-m\fR"Uses the pattern "^From " to split the file.  This is convenient forbreaking up a mailbox file.  It also changes the default outputpattern so that the output files are named according to the date andtime of each message.  This makes it easy to sort the messages in amailbox into chronological order \- just slice the box and cat theresulting files back together again..IP "\fB-s\fR"Uses the pattern "^#\&!\ */bin/sh" to split the file.  This is ideal forbreaking up mail or news article containing a Bourne shell script..IP "\fB-n\fIn\fR"Split the file every \fIn\fR lines.  In this case, no pattern matching is performed.  This is the behaviour of \fIsplit (1)\fR,except that the default output filename format for\fIslice\fR is different..IP "\fB-r\fR \fIrejectfile\fR"Establishes the name of a file to which rejected lines are sent(default is \fI/dev/null\fR).  Lines appearing before the first matchedpattern are rejected.  Also, any time an output file cannot be opened,source lines are sent to the reject file instead.  Substitutionparameters are not allowed in the reject file name, but a leading "+"is allowed (to indicated that the reject file should be opened for append).The name \fIstderr\fR can also be used for the standard error file..IP "\fB-e\fR \fIexpression\fR"Uses the pattern specified by \fIexpression\fR to identify where thefile is to be split.  The pattern may contain newlines (which matchthemselves).  See.I ed (1)for details of the regular expressions..IP \fIformat\fRAll three forms of the command allow the specification of zero or more\fIformat\fR strings as non-flag parameters.  Formats are used togenerate output filenames.  You can provide as many as you like.Every time a split is required, a format is used to generate the filename.  If the name is the same as the last name generated from thatformat, a new format is selected.  If \fIslice\fR runs out of formats,a warning will be issued (unless the last output file is \fIstdout\fR)and the remaining lines will be sent to the reject file.  The formatstrings can contain substitution parameters.  .SH SUBSTITUTIONS.IP "#\&f"The name of the input file, less any leading pathname, is substituted.If the input file is \fIstdin\fR, the name \fIslice\fR is used..IP "#\&n"A unique number, beginning with the value specified in the \fB-i\fRoption, is substituted.  This number is incremented by 1 for eachoutput file produced by the current output format.  When an outputformat produces the same name twice, a new format is selected andnumbering begins again with the initial value..IP "#\&1, #\&2 ..."Parameters of the form #\&1, #\&2, ... #\&9 are replaced by correspondingtokens drawn from the source line which matched the slice pattern.For example, if each procedure in a C program began with a commentline of the following form:.sp\ \ \ \ /\&* Procedure:\ \ \ main */.br\ \ \ \ #\&1\ \ \ #\&2\ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ #\&3\ \ #\&4 .IPthen parameter #\&3 could be used to extract the procedure name (inthis case, "main").  If #\&3 appears in an output format, it will bereplaced by this value..IP "#$"This parameter is used to select the last non-blank parameter on thematched line.  For example, if the matched line were:.sp\ \ \ \ \From garyp@cognos Tue Sep 15 15:08:23 EDT 1987.spthen "#$" would select "1987", the last token on the line..SH FORMAT SPEC's.LPSubstitution parameters can be followed by an optional .I printf style format specification.  They can be used to control the width ofthe field and formatting of a numeric token into a string..LPIf a numeric format like %d is used, the token is assumed to containan integer value and the function \fIatoi\fR is used to convert it.The resulting value is then formatted with \fIsprintf\fR using theformat code..LPFor example, the output format zzz.#\&n%02d will produce files of theform zzz.01, zzz.02, zzz.03, etc.  The #\&n is replaced with the currentcount value, which is then formatted by %02d (signed decimal integer,2 digits wide, print leading zeros)..LPFloating point format codes ("f", "e" and "g") are not allowed, nor is thesingle-character code "c"..LPThere is a special format code (not one of the usual \fIprintf\fR set)for conversion of 3 character month names to numbers.  The code "m"can be used to convert "Jan", "Feb", ...  "Dec" to the numbers 1, 2,\&... 12.  The numeric month value is then formatted as if the formatstring were of the %d type.  For example, the code %02m will result inthe month name token "Aug" being transformed into the string "08"..SH DEFAULT FORMATSIf no output formats are supplied, the following default format is used:.sp .5v\ \ \ \ #\&f:#\&n%03d.sp .5vThis results in files having names like \fIfilename\fR:001, \fIfilename\fR:002, \fIfilename\fR:003 and so on.  The default format was chosen because the resulting files are listed in numerical order by.I lsor by.I echo *which is sometimes useful..LPIf the \fB-m\fR option is used, the default output format is:.sp .5v\ \ \ \ #\&f:#\&$-#\&4%02m-#\&5%02d.#\&6.sp .5vwhich results in files have names like \fIfilename\fR:1987-01-25.08:15:30.The date and time are taken from the "From" line of each message inthe mailbox.  .LPYou can take advantage of this naming convention to reassemble themessages in chronological order by \fIcat\fR'ing the files backtogether again using the command:.sp\ \ \ \ cat \fIfilename\fR:*\ \  >\fImailbox.new\fR.SH SPECIAL CONVENTIONS.LPNormally, \fIslice\fR opens each output file for "write".  Byprepending an output format string with "+" (a plus sign) thedesignated output file will be opened for "append".  Thus, a filenamecan appear more than once..LPIf a format string is simply a "+", then \fIstdout\fR isused.  This is handy for piping one of the pieces to anothercommand.  .LPThe special format string "@" is used as an abbreviation for/dev/null.  This is handy for throwing away slices..LP.SH EXAMPLESSplit up a mail folder into a series of files, one for each message,named after the folder and date/time of each message..sp	slice -m -f FOLDER.spPipe a news article containing a shell script to slice, savingeverything before the line "#!/bin/sh" line to a file named HEADER andpiping the script portion to \fIsh\fR to unshar it:.sp	| slice -s -r HEADER +\ \ | sh.spSplit \fIstdin\fR at every line of dashes into the files 0, 1, 2, etc.:.sp	cat anyfile | slice -i0\ \  "^--* *$"\ \  #\&n.spPipe the middle portion of a file to sh, keeping the head and tail in afile called README.  Exclude the cut lines:.sp	slice -f myfile -x -r README "^--* [Cc]ut "\ \ +\ \ +README\ \ | sh.spKeep the middle portion of a file, discarding the head and tail:.sp	slice -f myfile -r @ "^--* [Cc]ut "\ \ middle @.spSplit a C program which has an identifying comment before eachprocedure into separate files for each procedure (named after theprocedure): .sp	slice -f program.c "/[*] *Procedure: *.* *[*]/"\ \  #\&3.c.sp.SH BUGS.IP a) 4Watch out for filename expansion by the shell.  This could cause\fIslice\fR to interpret the extra filenames as output formats causingfiles to be overwritten.  To be safe, do "set noclobber" in csh..IP b) 4When the -a option is used and an output format contains tokensubstitutions (#\&1, #\&2, etc.) the wrong filenames are generated.  Togenerate the correct filenames, either slice has to lookahead to findthe next match line or it has to direct lines for the current sliceinto a temporary file until it finds the line matching the pattern..SH DIAGNOSTICS``Internal Error'' indicates a bug in \fIslice\fR, and should be reported.Exit status 1 indicates an error parsing options \- for example, if an unknownflag was used.Exit status 2 indicates a meaningless combination was detected and rejected.Exit status 3 indicates a run-time problem \- for example, if a file couldn'tbe opened..LPIf a reject file is not provided, a count of rejected lines is reported..SH "SEE ALSO".I cat (1),.I ed (1),.I ls (1),.I mail (1),.I split (1),.I mailsplit (1L),.I atoi (3),.I printf (3).